From c02a8f0174818bdac880bf9dc25b11929b118518 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:34:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WoW64: MXCSR and the x87 status word through the 32-bit
 context

The WoW64 module never loaded MXCSR from the 32-bit context and never stored it (ReconstructWowContext even forced
0x1f80), and it carried only IE, C0-C3 and TOP of the x87 status word. With the floating-point exceptions of patch 0001
that hung 32-bit programs: an SSE exception unmasked with _controlfp trapped, the handler masked it in the context and
continued, and the JIT re-armed the trap from its own copy and trapped again, forever (x87test32 on the engine built by
CI). The MXCSR flags also did not survive the native code of a system call.

The context now carries MXCSR (control bits from the state, status flags from FPSR: the faulting context's for an
exception or suspend, the live one at a system call) and the whole status word, and loading it sets the state and FPSR.
An SSE exception that trapped on the host sets its flag in the context's MXCSR and is reported as
STATUS_FLOAT_MULTIPLE_TRAPS, which 32-bit Windows does for every SIMD floating-point exception (Windows Server 2022 and
2025; x64 programs get the specific code). The x87 status word conversion is now shared with the ARM64EC module.
x87test32 120/128 like the 64-bit build (the eight known x87 gaps), every _controlfp check passes.
